{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Jrk3jvVX4nS8GRrDXHBTdt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the data:\n- Function-level: classification F1 87.1/87.9, CWM F1 87.31/87.71 — essentially identical (null result confirmed)\n- The paper's core bet is now on repo-level complexity-dependent advantage\n- Oracle sim shows: per-test advantage concentrates in multi-F2P instances, P2P regression rate ~1% (too low to help), block correlation matters a lot\n- SWE-bench F2P stats: median T=1, mean T=3, 68.7% are T=1\n\n\n- The remaining critical experiments are multi-seed-core, trace-then-judge, repo-level Docker evaluation, and diagnostic re-repair\n\nNow I'm mapping out the outcome scenarios. In the best case, repo-level evaluation shows clear classification advantage over CWM on longer traces, diagnostic re-repair yields meaningful improvements, and block correlation validates empirically — the paper's framing holds. The median scenario has repo-level showing modest or mixed results with marginal re-repair gains, requiring me to reframe Claim 1 around efficiency and downgrade Claim 2 to a hypothesis. The worst case is if repo-level also fails to show advantage.\n\nI'm setting concrete success thresholds for each experiment. For repo-level, I'm looking for at least a 5-point F1 gap with CWM showing 15%+ truncation issues in the best case, 2-5 points with visible truncation problems in the median case, and less than 2 points in the worst case. Diagnostic re-repair needs 5%+ absolute improvement to be strong, 1-4% for median, and under 1% signals failure. Multi-seed at function-level should show classification and CWM performing equivalently across all scenarios.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how variance affects the equivalence claim—tight confidence intervals make the equivalence argument strongest, moderate variance is still workable, but high variance undermines even the equivalence finding.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10255,"cache_read_input_tokens":11701,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10255},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd31d16a-fe80-488c-bd84-da386c430aa7","uuid":"a336b4ae-a9f5-4543-9d3d-0041c50130dd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Jrk3jvVX4nS8GRrDXHBTdt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Repo-level validates complexity-dependent advantage; diagnostic re-repair shows practical impact; full 3-claim story holds\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"CWM generation suffers token-budget exhaustion / string-value errors on repo-level traces (unparseable+truncated ≥15%)\"\n      - \"Classification maintains ≥80% F1 on repo-level while CWM degrades significantly\"\n      - \"Per-test diagnostic signal translates to measurable re-repair resolve rate gain\"\n      - \"Block-correlated error structure empirically confirmed, amplifying per-test advantage\"\n      - \"Multi-seed function-level confirms tight equivalence (CI overlap), cleanly supporting null result\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_cls_vs_cwm_f1_diff_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_multi_seed_std: [0.2, 0.6]\n      repo_level_cls_f1: [78, 85]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [65, 75]\n      repo_level_gap_pp: [5, 15]\n      cwm_repo_unparseable_or_truncated_pct: [15, 35]\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_rate_gain_pp: [3, 8]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_direct_cls_f1_diff: [-1.5, 1.5]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Original framing holds. Title: complexity-dependent reformulation advantage.\n      Function-level equivalence is the setup (classification is not trivially easier),\n      repo-level gap is the punchline (CWM failure modes emerge at complexity).\n      Diagnostic re-repair validates Claim 2 as empirical finding.\n      Oracle frontier (Claim 3) provides deployment decision framework.\n      Target: EMNLP Main. All three claims as stated.\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Repo-level shows modest/noisy advantage; diagnostic signal exists but re-repair gain is marginal; paper narrows to Claims 1+3 with Claim 2 as hypothesis\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"CWM shows some degradation on repo-level but not catastrophic (unparseable 5-14%)\"\n      - \"Classification has small but not clearly significant repo-level advantage\"\n      - \"Re-repair experiment shows directional but non-significant improvement\"\n      - \"Function-level null result replicates cleanly across seeds\"\n      - \"Block correlation partially confirmed or effect size smaller than simulated\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_cls_vs_cwm_f1_diff_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_multi_seed_std: [0.3, 1.0]\n      repo_level_cls_f1: [72, 80]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [68, 77]\n      repo_level_gap_pp: [2, 5]\n      cwm_repo_unparseable_or_truncated_pct: [5, 14]\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_rate_gain_pp: [0.5, 3]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_direct_cls_f1_diff: [-2, 2]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Reframe Claim 1 as efficiency-equivalent accuracy at function-level + moderate\n      complexity-dependent advantage at repo-level (\"classification matches generation\n      accuracy while using 1 token vs N tokens; advantage grows with output complexity\n      but the threshold is higher than initially hypothesized\"). Claim 2 downgrades\n      to motivated hypothesis with directional evidence, not validated finding.\n      Claim 3 (oracle frontier) becomes co-primary contribution — the characterization\n      of when per-test verification helps is the strongest standalone result.\n      Paper weight shifts to 60% characterization + 40% method.\n      Target: EMNLP Findings or Main with strong rebuttal prep.\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Repo-level also null; no downstream benefit; paper pivots to empirical analysis of verification formulation equivalence + oracle characterization\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"CWM handles repo-level adequately (Qwen3-4B learns to compress/summarize traces)\"\n      - \"Classification ≈ generation at both function-level AND repo-level\"\n      - \"Re-repair shows no significant improvement from per-test diagnostics\"\n      - \"Block correlation effect weaker than simulated — real errors closer to i.i.d.\"\n      - \"T=1 dominance (68.7%) limits per-test advantage even in oracle setting\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_cls_vs_cwm_f1_diff_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_multi_seed_std: [0.5, 1.5]\n      repo_level_cls_f1: [70, 78]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [69, 78]\n      repo_level_gap_pp: [-1, 2]\n      cwm_repo_unparseable_or_truncated_pct: [0, 5]\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_rate_gain_pp: [-0.5, 1]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_direct_cls_f1_diff: [-2, 2]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Abandon method-superiority framing entirely. Pivot to analysis paper:\n      \"Are Classification and Generation Equivalent for Code Execution Simulation?\n      An Empirical Study.\" Core contribution becomes the surprising negative result\n      itself — across function-level AND repo-level, reformulation doesn't matter,\n      which challenges the intuition that generation is harder. Secondary contribution:\n      oracle frontier characterization showing per-test granularity is theoretically\n      valuable only under specific conditions (multi-F2P, block-correlated errors,\n      regression rate >10%) that rarely co-occur in practice. Efficiency angle\n      (1-token inference) becomes a minor practical note, not a claim.\n      Claim 2 dropped. Claim 3 reframed as \"negative characterization\" — here's\n      why per-test doesn't help in practice despite theoretical appeal.\n      Target: EMNLP Findings or pivot to ICSE 2027 as empirical study.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10255,"cache_read_input_tokens":11701,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10255},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd31d16a-fe80-488c-bd84-da386c430aa7","uuid":"94de4220-2e92-4fed-9cbc-c1f9ab72f425"}
